MKDIR(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     MKDIR(1)

NAME
    mkdir — Create a directory, because eventually you have to put
            all those files somewhere.

SYNOPSIS
    mkdir <dir>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'mkdir' command creates a new directory at the specified path.

    Parent directories are created automatically when necessary.

    In other words, if you ask for:

        mkdir projects/2026/important-stuff

    and 'projects' and '2026' don't exist yet, this command will happily
    build the entire family tree for you.

    However, if the target already exists, 'mkdir' refuses to overwrite
    it and politely informs you that you're late to the party.

ARGUMENTS
    dir
        The directory to create.

        Required. If omitted, the command prints its usage and creates
        nothing.

BEHAVIOR
    Parent directories are created automatically.

    The target directory itself must not already exist.

    Existing paths are left completely alone. This is one of the rare
    commands in this CLI that exercises restraint.

OUTPUT
    On successful creation:

        Created <path>

    If the directory already exists:

        Already exists: <path>

    No destruction occurred. Everyone can relax.

ERRORS
    mkdir error: <error>
        Something else went wrong while creating the directory.

        Possible causes include:
        - Permission denied.
        - An intermediate path is not a directory.
        - The filesystem is being difficult.
        - You attempted something ambitious before having coffee.

EXAMPLES
    > mkdir projects
        Created projects

        A place to put things. Very organized of you.

    > mkdir projects/2026
        Creates '2026' inside 'projects'.

        Assuming 'projects' already exists.

    > mkdir projects/2026/important
        Creates all missing parent directories along the way.

        One command. Several directories. Maximum efficiency.

    > mkdir already-here
        Already exists: already-here

        The directory got there first.

    > mkdir
        Usage: mkdir <dir>

        You forgot to tell it what to build.

FUN FACTS
    - Parent directories are created automatically.
    - Existing targets are never silently overwritten.
    - 'mkdir' uses exclusive creation: if something is already there,
      it says so.
    - Unlike 'rmdir', this command is generally considered constructive.
    - Every great filesystem empire begins with a single 'mkdir'.

SEE ALSO
    rmdir(1), cd(1), ls(1), pwd(1)

